-- California Business and Professions Code, Chapter 25.1 (Transparency in Frontier
-- Artificial Intelligence Act): section 22757.13, critical safety incident reporting,
-- with the act's uncodified carve-outs (SB 53, SEC. 5(d), (e)) and the general
-- California rules for counting days (Code Civ. Proc. §§ 12, 12a).
--
-- DRAFT. Not for use as advice. No domain expert and no lawyer qualified in
-- California law has read this against the source.
--
-- Seat: a frontier developer. Where a subdivision addresses only the Office of
-- Emergency Services or the Attorney General, it is encoded for what it means to the
-- developer, or recorded as inert.
-- Sources: ../../registers/source-bundle/ca-bpc-div8-ch25-1-tfaia-2026-10.txt
-- (`src:N`), ca-time-computation-2026-10.txt (`src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:N`)
-- ca-sb53-stats-2025-ch-138-chaptered.txt (`src:ca-sb53-stats-2025-ch-138-chaptered:N`) and
-- ca-time-computation-holidays-2026-10.txt (`src:ca-time-computation-holidays-2026-10:N`).
-- Target compiler: main. Forks F1 to F16 are in NOTES.md.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `tfaia-domain`
IMPORT `tfaia-definitions`

§ `When the chapter does not apply to me`

-- SEC. 5 of the act is not codified. (d) and (e) switch the act off "to the extent"
-- of a strict conflict with a federal-government contract, or of federal preemption.
-- Whether either is so is a fact the developer states (with its lawyers); the
-- encoding never decides preemption (F11). (a) to (c) and (f) are inert here.
-- The date the chapter took effect is not in SEC. 5: it is the Legislative Counsel's
-- history note on each section of Chapter 25.1 ("Effective January 1, 2026").
-- src:ca-sb53-stats-2025-ch-138-chaptered:235 | SEC. 5.
-- src:ca-sb53-stats-2025-ch-138-chaptered:236 | (a) The provisions of this act are severable. If any provision of this act or its application is held invalid, that invalidity shall not affect other provisions or applications that can be given effect without the invalid provision or application.
-- src:ca-sb53-stats-2025-ch-138-chaptered:237 | (b) This act shall be liberally construed to effectuate its purposes.
-- src:ca-sb53-stats-2025-ch-138-chaptered:238 | (c) The duties and obligations imposed by this act are cumulative with any other duties or obligations imposed under other law and shall not be construed to relieve any party from any duties or obligations imposed under other law and do not limit any rights or remedies under existing law.
-- src:ca-sb53-stats-2025-ch-138-chaptered:239 | (d) This act shall not apply to the extent that it strictly conflicts with the terms of a contract between a federal government entity and a frontier developer.
-- src:ca-sb53-stats-2025-ch-138-chaptered:240 | (e) This act shall not apply to the extent that it is preempted by federal law.
-- src:ca-sb53-stats-2025-ch-138-chaptered:241 | (f) This act preempts any rule, regulation, code, ordinance, or other law adopted by a city, county, city and county, municipality, or local agency on or after January 1, 2025, specifically related to the regulation of frontier developers with respect to their management of catastrophic risk.
@ref SB 53 (Stats. 2025, ch. 138), SEC. 5 (src:ca-sb53-stats-2025-ch-138-chaptered:235-241); effective date from the history note to Bus. & Prof. Code §§ 22757.10-22757.16
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the chapter took effect` MEANS Date 1 1 2026

-- The checks that switch the chapter off, in one place, so that each answer gives the
-- same reason.
DECLARE `whether the chapter reaches the incident` IS ONE OF
    `the chapter reaches it`
    `not in force or preempted`
    `a strictly conflicting federal-government contract`
    `discovered before the chapter took effect`

@ref SB 53, SEC. 5(d), (e) (src:ca-sb53-stats-2025-ch-138-chaptered:239-240); history note to § 22757.13, effective January 1, 2026
GIVEN p IS A `my reporting position`
GIVETH A `whether the chapter reaches the incident`
`whether the chapter reaches` p MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT p's `the chapter is in force and not preempted as applied to me`)
            THEN `not in force or preempted`
        IF p's `a contract between a federal government entity and me strictly conflicts with the reporting duty`
            THEN `a strictly conflicting federal-government contract`
        IF p's `the date I discovered the critical safety incident` LESS THAN `the day the chapter took effect`
            THEN `discovered before the chapter took effect`
        OTHERWISE `the chapter reaches it`

§ `Section 22757.13(a) -- what a report gives`

-- src:84 | Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13.
-- src:85 | (a) The Office of Emergency Services shall establish a mechanism to be used by a frontier developer or a member of the public to report a critical safety incident that includes all of the following:
-- src:86 | (1) The date of the critical safety incident.
-- src:87 | (2) The reasons the incident qualifies as a critical safety incident.
-- src:88 | (3) A short and plain statement describing the critical safety incident.
-- src:89 | (4) Whether the incident was associated with internal use of a frontier model.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(a) (src:84-89)
GIVETH A LIST OF `an item of a critical safety incident report`
`the items a critical safety incident report gives` MEANS
    LIST `item 1, the date of the critical safety incident`
       , `item 2, the reasons the incident qualifies as a critical safety incident`
       , `item 3, a short and plain statement describing the critical safety incident`
       , `item 4, whether the incident was associated with internal use of a frontier model`

-- (b) is about large frontier developers' summaries of internal-use risk assessments
-- (section 22757.12(d)), not about incidents: out of scope. (b)(2) is the Office's
-- duty to limit access to reports about internal use: inert.
-- src:90 | (b) (1) The Office of Emergency Services shall establish a mechanism to be used by a large frontier developer to confidentially submit summaries of any assessments of the potential for catastrophic risk resulting from internal use of its frontier models.
-- src:91 | (2) The Office of Emergency Services shall take all necessary precautions to limit access to any reports related to internal use of frontier models to only personnel with a specific need to know the information and to protect the reports from unauthorized access.

§ `Section 22757.13(c)(1) -- the 15-day report to the Office of Emergency Services`

§§ `Counting the 15 days`

-- Chapter 25.1 states no rule for counting its days. Code Civ. Proc. § 12 (and the
-- same words in Civ. Code § 10 and Gov. Code § 6800): exclude the first day, include
-- the last. Code Civ. Proc. § 12a: if the last day is a holiday, the period runs to the
-- next day that is not a holiday; "holiday" means every Saturday, the holidays in
-- § 135 and the days § 12b makes holidays; and § 12a applies to "all other provisions
-- of law ... whether expressed in this or any other code" (F9). Saturdays and Sundays
-- are computed below; the developer lists any other holiday.
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:10 | Code Civ. Proc. § 12.
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:11 | The time in which any act provided by law is to be done is computed by excluding the first day, and including the last, unless the last day is a holiday, and then it is also excluded.
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:12 | [Enacted 1872.]
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:14 | Code Civ. Proc. § 12a.
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:15 | (a) If the last day for the performance of any act provided or required by law to be performed within a specified period of time is a holiday, then that period is hereby extended to and including the next day that is not a holiday. For purposes of this section, “holiday” means all day on Saturdays, all holidays specified in Section 135 and, to the extent provided in Section 12b, all days that by terms of Section 12b are required to be considered as holidays.
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:16 | (b) This section applies to Sections 659, 659a, and 921, and to all other provisions of law providing or requiring an act to be performed on a particular day or within a specified period of time, whether expressed in this or any other code or statute, ordinance, rule, or regulation.
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:17 | [Amended by Stats. 2007, Ch. 263, Sec. 3. Effective January 1, 2008.]
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:19 | Civ. Code § 10.
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:20 | The time in which any act provided by law is to be done is computed by excluding the first day and including the last, unless the last day is a holiday, and then it is also excluded.
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:21 | [Enacted 1872.]
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:23 | Gov. Code § 6800.
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:24 | The time in which any act provided by law is to be done is computed by excluding the first day, and including the last, unless the last day is a holiday, and then it is also excluded.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. §§ 12, 12a; Civ. Code § 10; Gov. Code § 6800 (src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:10-24)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days to report` MEANS 15

-- "holiday" in § 12a(a) is every Saturday, the holidays in § 135 and the days § 12b makes
-- holidays. § 135 makes a holiday of "Every full day designated as a holiday by Section
-- 6700 of the Government Code", and Gov. Code § 6700(a)(1) designates "Every Sunday".
-- Saturdays and Sundays are computed here. No other holiday is: the rest of § 6700 (less
-- § 135's exceptions) and § 12b's closed-office days come from the developer's list, so
-- with an empty list the answer is the EARLIEST possible last day, assuming no other
-- holiday (F9). Fixed-date and nth-weekday holidays (1 January, Thanksgiving and the
-- day after, ...) are not computed. Vintage: the deposited § 6700 took effect 2026-03-26;
-- Sundays are computed for earlier dates too, resting on that later text (F9).
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:15 | (a) If the last day for the performance of any act provided or required by law to be performed within a specified period of time is a holiday, then that period is hereby extended to and including the next day that is not a holiday. For purposes of this section, “holiday” means all day on Saturdays, all holidays specified in Section 135 and, to the extent provided in Section 12b, all days that by terms of Section 12b are required to be considered as holidays.
-- src:ca-time-computation-holidays-2026-10:16 | Every full day designated as a holiday by Section 6700 of the Government Code, including that Thursday of November declared by the President to be Thanksgiving Day, is a judicial holiday, except the date corresponding with the second new moon following the winter solstice, or the third new moon following the winter solstice should an intercalary month intervene, known as “Lunar New Year,” the 15th day of the month of Kartik in the Hindu lunar calendar of each year, known as “Diwali,” April 24, known as “Genocide Remembrance Day,” September 9, known as “Admission Day,” the second Monday in October, known as “Columbus Day,” and any other day appointed by the President, but not by the Governor, for a public fast, thanksgiving, or holiday. If a judicial holiday falls on a Saturday or a Sunday, the Judicial Council may designate an alternative day for observance of the holiday. Every Saturday and the day after Thanksgiving Day are judicial holidays. Officers and employees of the courts shall observe only the judicial holidays established pursuant to this section.
-- src:ca-time-computation-holidays-2026-10:20 | (a) The holidays in this state are:
-- src:ca-time-computation-holidays-2026-10:21 | (1) Every Sunday.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a) (src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:15); Code Civ. Proc. § 135; Gov. Code § 6700(a)(1) (src:ca-time-computation-holidays-2026-10:16-21)
GIVEN d IS A DATE, `other holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is a holiday` d `other holidays` MEANS
       (`Weekday of` d) EQUALS Saturday
    OR (`Weekday of` d) EQUALS Sunday
    OR elem d `other holidays`

@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a) (src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:15)
GIVEN d IS A DATE, `other holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the next day that is not a holiday, from` d `other holidays` MEANS
    IF `is a holiday` d `other holidays`
    THEN `the next day that is not a holiday, from` (d PLUS 1) `other holidays`
    ELSE d

-- src:92 | (c) (1) Subject to paragraph (2), a frontier developer shall report any critical safety incident pertaining to one or more of its frontier models to the Office of Emergency Services within 15 days of discovering the critical safety incident.
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:11 | The time in which any act provided by law is to be done is computed by excluding the first day, and including the last, unless the last day is a holiday, and then it is also excluded.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(c)(1) (src:92); Code Civ. Proc. § 12 (src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:11)
GIVEN `the day of discovery` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the 15th day after discovery` `the day of discovery` MEANS
    `the day of discovery` PLUS `the days to report`

-- src:92 | (c) (1) Subject to paragraph (2), a frontier developer shall report any critical safety incident pertaining to one or more of its frontier models to the Office of Emergency Services within 15 days of discovering the critical safety incident.
-- src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:15 | (a) If the last day for the performance of any act provided or required by law to be performed within a specified period of time is a holiday, then that period is hereby extended to and including the next day that is not a holiday. For purposes of this section, “holiday” means all day on Saturdays, all holidays specified in Section 135 and, to the extent provided in Section 12b, all days that by terms of Section 12b are required to be considered as holidays.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(c)(1) (src:92); Code Civ. Proc. § 12a (src:ca-time-computation-2026-10:15)
GIVEN `the day of discovery` IS A DATE, `other holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to report to the Office` `the day of discovery` `other holidays` MEANS
    `the next day that is not a holiday, from` (`the 15th day after discovery` `the day of discovery`) `other holidays`

-- A report made on or before the last day is in time.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(c)(1) (src:92)
GIVEN `the day of discovery` IS A DATE, `the day I reported` IS A DATE, `other holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`I reported to the Office in time` `the day of discovery` `the day I reported` `other holidays` MEANS
    `the day I reported` AT MOST `the last day to report to the Office` `the day of discovery` `other holidays`

§§ `Who must report what`

-- "a frontier developer shall report any critical safety incident pertaining to one or
-- more of its frontier models". Every frontier developer, large or not (F1). Whether
-- the model has been deployed does not matter (F7). "discovering" is not defined; the
-- developer gives the date it discovered the incident (F10). "Subject to paragraph (2)"
-- is read as leaving this duty in place beside the 24-hour one (F4).
-- (c)(4): a frontier developer "is encouraged, but not required" to report incidents
-- with its foundation models that are not frontier models. By (d) every kind of
-- critical safety incident involves a frontier model, so (c)(4) is read as speaking of
-- events that would be critical safety incidents if the model were a frontier model (F8).
-- (i): after a declaration of intent, the developer is "deemed in compliance with this
-- section to the extent" it meets a federal standard the Office has designated by
-- regulation, and failing that standard "shall constitute a violation of this chapter".
-- src:92 | (c) (1) Subject to paragraph (2), a frontier developer shall report any critical safety incident pertaining to one or more of its frontier models to the Office of Emergency Services within 15 days of discovering the critical safety incident.
-- src:95 | (4) A frontier developer is encouraged, but not required, to report critical safety incidents pertaining to foundation models that are not frontier models.
-- src:103 | (h) The Office of Emergency Services may adopt regulations designating one or more federal laws, regulations, or guidance documents that meet all of the following conditions for the purposes of subdivision (i):
-- src:104 | (1) (A) The law, regulation, or guidance document imposes or states standards or requirements for critical safety incident reporting that are substantially equivalent to, or stricter than, those required by this section.
-- src:105 | (B) The law, regulation, or guidance document described in subparagraph (A) does not need to require critical safety incident reporting to the State of California.
-- src:106 | (2) The law, regulation, or guidance document is intended to assess, detect, or mitigate the catastrophic risk.
-- src:107 | (i) (1) A frontier developer that intends to comply with this section by complying with the requirements of, or meeting the standards stated by, a federal law, regulation, or guidance document designated pursuant to subdivision (h) shall declare its intent to do so to the Office of Emergency Services.
-- src:108 | (2) After a frontier developer has declared its intent pursuant to paragraph (1), both of the following apply:
-- src:109 | (A) The frontier developer shall be deemed in compliance with this section to the extent that the frontier developer meets the standards of, or complies with the requirements imposed or stated by, the designated federal law, regulation, or guidance document until the frontier developer declares the revocation of that intent to the Office of Emergency Services or the Office of Emergency Services revokes a relevant regulation pursuant to subdivision (j).
-- src:110 | (B) The failure by a frontier developer to meet the standards of, or comply with the requirements stated by, the federal law, regulation, or guidance document designated pursuant to subdivision (h) shall constitute a violation of this chapter.
-- src:111 | (j) The Office of Emergency Services shall revoke a regulation adopted under subdivision (h) if the requirements of subdivision (h) are no longer met.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(c)(1), (c)(4), (h) to (j) (src:92-111)
DECLARE `my duty to report to the Office of Emergency Services` IS ONE OF
    `not covered, because I have said the chapter is not in force, or is preempted, as applied to me`
    `not covered, because a contract between a federal government entity and me strictly conflicts with the reporting duty`
    `not covered, because I discovered the incident before the chapter took effect on 1 January 2026`
    `no duty, because I am not a frontier developer`
    `not required but encouraged, because the model is a foundation model of mine that is not a frontier model`
    `no duty, because the model is not a frontier model of mine`
    `no duty, because the incident is not a critical safety incident`
    `deemed in compliance to the extent I meet the designated federal standard, and failing it is a violation of the chapter`
    `report it to the Office of Emergency Services` HAS
        `the 15th day after discovery`                    IS A DATE
        `the last day, after any extension for holidays`  IS A DATE

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(c)(4) (src:95); § 22757.11(d), (f), (i)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`, i IS A `the incident as I know it`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the incident is one (c)(4) encourages me to report` m i IF
        `I am a frontier developer` m
    AND m's `I have trained, or initiated the training of, the model`
    AND `the model is a foundation model` m
    AND (NOT `the model is a frontier model` m)
    AND (   `the facts fit kind 1` i
         OR `the facts fit kind 2` i
         OR `the facts fit kind 3` i
         OR `the facts fit kind 4` i)

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(c)(1), (c)(4), (i)(2) (src:92) (src:95) (src:108-110)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`, i IS A `the incident as I know it`, p IS A `my reporting position`, `other holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A `my duty to report to the Office of Emergency Services`
`my duty to report to the Office` m i p `other holidays` MEANS
    CONSIDER `whether the chapter reaches` p
    WHEN `not in force or preempted`
        THEN `not covered, because I have said the chapter is not in force, or is preempted, as applied to me`
    WHEN `a strictly conflicting federal-government contract`
        THEN `not covered, because a contract between a federal government entity and me strictly conflicts with the reporting duty`
    WHEN `discovered before the chapter took effect`
        THEN `not covered, because I discovered the incident before the chapter took effect on 1 January 2026`
    WHEN `the chapter reaches it` THEN
        BRANCH
            IF (NOT `I am a frontier developer` m)
                THEN `no duty, because I am not a frontier developer`
            IF `the incident is one (c)(4) encourages me to report` m i
                THEN `not required but encouraged, because the model is a foundation model of mine that is not a frontier model`
            IF (NOT `the model is a frontier model of mine` m)
                THEN `no duty, because the model is not a frontier model of mine`
            IF (NOT `it is a critical safety incident` m i)
                THEN `no duty, because the incident is not a critical safety incident`
            IF p's `my declaration under section 22757.13(i) is in effect`
                THEN `deemed in compliance to the extent I meet the designated federal standard, and failing it is a violation of the chapter`
            OTHERWISE `report it to the Office of Emergency Services` WITH
                `the 15th day after discovery`                   IS `the 15th day after discovery` (p's `the date I discovered the critical safety incident`)
                `the last day, after any extension for holidays` IS `the last day to report to the Office` (p's `the date I discovered the critical safety incident`) `other holidays`

§ `Section 22757.13(c)(2) -- disclosure within 24 hours`

-- "If a frontier developer discovers that a critical safety incident poses an imminent
-- risk of death or serious physical injury": unlike (c)(1), the words do not limit this
-- to incidents with the developer's own frontier models; read literally (F12). The 24
-- hours are counted from the discovery of the imminent risk (F10); a disclosure at
-- exactly 24 hours is within them. The recipient is "an authority, including any law
-- enforcement agency or public safety agency with jurisdiction, that is appropriate
-- based on the nature of that incident and as required by law"; the encoding does not
-- name one. Code Civ. Proc. §§ 12 and 12a count days, not hours, and are not applied.
-- src:93 | (2) If a frontier developer discovers that a critical safety incident poses an imminent risk of death or serious physical injury, the frontier developer shall disclose that incident within 24 hours to an authority, including any law enforcement agency or public safety agency with jurisdiction, that is appropriate based on the nature of that incident and as required by law.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(c)(2) (src:93)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the hours to disclose` MEANS 24

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(c)(2), (i)(2) (src:93)
DECLARE `my duty to disclose within 24 hours` IS ONE OF
    `not covered, because I have said the chapter is not in force, or is preempted, as applied to me, for the 24-hour disclosure`
    `not covered, because a contract between a federal government entity and me strictly conflicts with the 24-hour disclosure`
    `not covered, because I discovered the incident before the chapter took effect on 1 January 2026, for the 24-hour disclosure`
    `no 24-hour duty, because I am not a frontier developer`
    `no 24-hour duty, because the incident is not a critical safety incident`
    `no 24-hour duty, because I have not discovered that the incident poses an imminent risk of death or serious physical injury`
    `deemed in compliance with the 24-hour disclosure to the extent I meet the designated federal standard, and failing it is a violation of the chapter`
    `disclose it within 24 hours to an appropriate authority, such as a law enforcement or public safety agency with jurisdiction`

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(c)(2), (i)(2) (src:93)
GIVEN m IS A `me and my model`, i IS A `the incident as I know it`, p IS A `my reporting position`
GIVETH A `my duty to disclose within 24 hours`
`my duty to disclose within 24 hours under (c)(2)` m i p MEANS
    CONSIDER `whether the chapter reaches` p
    WHEN `not in force or preempted`
        THEN `not covered, because I have said the chapter is not in force, or is preempted, as applied to me, for the 24-hour disclosure`
    WHEN `a strictly conflicting federal-government contract`
        THEN `not covered, because a contract between a federal government entity and me strictly conflicts with the 24-hour disclosure`
    WHEN `discovered before the chapter took effect`
        THEN `not covered, because I discovered the incident before the chapter took effect on 1 January 2026, for the 24-hour disclosure`
    WHEN `the chapter reaches it` THEN
        BRANCH
            IF (NOT `I am a frontier developer` m)
                THEN `no 24-hour duty, because I am not a frontier developer`
            IF (NOT `it is a critical safety incident` m i)
                THEN `no 24-hour duty, because the incident is not a critical safety incident`
            IF (NOT p's `I have discovered that the incident poses an imminent risk of death or serious physical injury`)
                THEN `no 24-hour duty, because I have not discovered that the incident poses an imminent risk of death or serious physical injury`
            IF p's `my declaration under section 22757.13(i) is in effect`
                THEN `deemed in compliance with the 24-hour disclosure to the extent I meet the designated federal standard, and failing it is a violation of the chapter`
            OTHERWISE `disclose it within 24 hours to an appropriate authority, such as a law enforcement or public safety agency with jurisdiction`

@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(c)(2) (src:93)
GIVEN `hours from discovering the imminent risk to my disclosure` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`I disclosed within 24 hours` `hours from discovering the imminent risk to my disclosure` MEANS
    `hours from discovering the imminent risk to my disclosure` AT MOST `the hours to disclose`

§ `Section 22757.13(c)(3), (c)(4) -- amended reports, and reports that are only encouraged`

-- src:94 | (3) A frontier developer that discovers information about a critical safety incident after filing the initial report required by this subdivision may file an amended report.
-- src:95 | (4) A frontier developer is encouraged, but not required, to report critical safety incidents pertaining to foundation models that are not frontier models.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(c)(3) (src:94-95)
GIVEN `I have filed my initial report` IS A BOOLEAN, `I discovered information about the incident after filing it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`I may file an amended report` `I have filed my initial report` `I discovered information about the incident after filing it` MEANS
        `I have filed my initial report`
    AND `I discovered information about the incident after filing it`

§ `Section 22757.13(d) to (g) -- what happens to my report`

-- (d) the Office reviews reports; (e) the Attorney General or the Office may pass
-- reports to the Legislature, the Governor, the federal government or state agencies,
-- "strongly" considering trade-secret, safety, cybersecurity and security risks;
-- (g) the Office's annual anonymized, aggregated report from 1 January 2027. Inert:
-- each is an official's act. (f) is encoded: the report is exempt from the California
-- Public Records Act.
-- src:96 | (d) The Office of Emergency Services shall review critical safety incident reports submitted by frontier developers and may review reports submitted by members of the public.
-- src:97 | (e) (1) The Attorney General or the Office of Emergency Services may transmit reports of critical safety incidents and reports from covered employees made pursuant to Chapter 5.1 (commencing with Section 1107) of Part 3 of Division 2 of the Labor Code to the Legislature, the Governor, the federal government, or appropriate state agencies.
-- src:98 | (2) The Attorney General or the Office of Emergency Services shall strongly consider any risks related to trade secrets, public safety, cybersecurity of a frontier developer, or national security when transmitting reports.
-- src:99 | (f) A report of a critical safety incident submitted to the Office of Emergency Services pursuant to this section, a report of assessments of catastrophic risk from internal use pursuant to Section 22757.12, and a covered employee report made pursuant to Chapter 5.1 (commencing with Section 1107) of Part 3 of Division 2 of the Labor Code are exempt from the California Public Records Act (Division 10 (commencing with Section 7920.000) of Title 1 of the Government Code).
-- src:100 | (g) (1) Beginning January 1, 2027, and annually thereafter, the Office of Emergency Services shall produce a report with anonymized and aggregated information about critical safety incidents that have been reviewed by the Office of Emergency Services since the preceding report.
-- src:101 | (2) The Office of Emergency Services shall not include information in a report pursuant to this subdivision that would compromise the trade secrets or cybersecurity of a frontier developer, public safety, or the national security of the United States or that would be prohibited by any federal or state law.
-- src:102 | (3) The Office of Emergency Services shall transmit a report pursuant to this subdivision to the Legislature, pursuant to Section 9795, and to the Governor.
@ref Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13(f) (src:96-102)
GIVEN `I submitted it to the Office under section 22757.13` IS A BOOLEAN
